# Vote

Default to preserving the finding. A wrong Reject permanently hides a real
bug; a wrong Promote/Uncertain only costs a downstream review. Reject ONLY on
affirmative disproof.

Unreachable trigger state under (a)-(f), or an exact consequence disproved
under (g), are the ONLY reasons that may reject a finding here. Do not invent
secondary reasons, and do not let several weak signals add up to a Reject.
In particular, the report's self-declared fields (`caller_contract`,
`parameter_control`, `trigger_source`, …) are NOT evidence: a real bug very
often shows `caller_contract: violated` precisely because enforcing that
contract on untrusted input is the LIBRARY's job. Reject only when you have
READ the source and can name either the specific constructor or guard that
makes the triggering state unreachable from every attacker entry, or the exact
source invariant that contradicts the claimed security consequence. Whenever
two readings are defensible, keep the finding (Uncertain).

Every Promote or Reject must include at least one verifiable source anchor.
An anchor cites one exact line in the audited tree: a target-relative path and
1-based line, a symbol or section name that occurs in that file, an anchor kind
(`source`, `contract`, or `build`), and the cited line with surrounding
whitespace removed. The harness verifies those fields, computes and persists
the excerpt SHA-256, and downgrades an unverifiable Promote or Reject to
Uncertain.

Output ONE JSON object on a single line, nothing else:

```json
{"vote":"Promote|Reject|Uncertain","rationale":"<one paragraph>","trigger_path":"<the attacker-reachable path you constructed, or the empty string>","disproof":"<for Reject only: file:function of the blocking invariant, plus which of (a)-(g) applies>","rejection_kind":"contract-invalid|unreachable|nonshipping|no-added-boundary|consequence-disproved|unknown","surface_applies":"yes|no","review_mode":"memory-contract|resource|disclosure|semantic-boundary","vulnerable_boundary_surface":"network|library-api|file-format|cli|dev-tool|internal|unknown","reproducer_carrier":"network|library-api|file-format|cli|harness|runner|unknown","trigger_controls_fit":"within|outside|unclear","anchors":[{"path":"relative/path.c","line":123,"symbol":"function_or_section","kind":"source|contract|build","excerpt":"exact trimmed source line"}]}
```

Definitions:
- **Promote**: you constructed a concrete attacker-reachable path (bytes or a
  public call sequence) into the triggering state, and source supports the
  report's exact claimed security consequence. Reachability alone is not a
  Promote.
- **Reject**: affirmative disproof — every route into the triggering state is
  blocked by a named, enforced invariant (cite it), OR the trigger requires a
  forged object / internal-only mutator with no public caller, OR the location
  does not ship, OR source/build evidence shows a trusted caller/operator
  extension surface or private metadata prerequisite (one of (a)-(f)), OR
  source affirmatively disproves the exact consequence under (g). Use
  `consequence-disproved` only for (g). Name the code or build evidence; do not
  reject on absence of a path you simply could not find. Do not use
  `unreachable` merely because a demonstrated,
  contract-obeying public API call is outside configured `attacker_controls`;
  that result is Promote as a real target defect, recorded through
  `trigger_controls_fit` below.
- **Uncertain**: you can neither construct nor affirmatively disprove the
  trigger or exact consequence in the budget, OR the surface is not in scope
  for this challenge. Name what would settle it. The defect is preserved on
  disk and reported as unadjudicated: it earns no security credit, and nothing
  claims it is out of scope either. Do not use Uncertain to park a case you did
  establish — say so through the vote and `trigger_controls_fit`.
- `vulnerable_boundary_surface` names what consumes the untrusted operation;
  `reproducer_carrier` names how the reproducer delivered it. They may differ.
  Never infer `file-format` from `cli + bytes`; use source/config evidence.
- `trigger_controls_fit` is your own threat-model comparison, read from source.
  List every component the fault actually requires, then answer **within** only
  when the configured controls cover ALL of them, and **outside** when even one
  falls outside the list. Crafted bytes mattering is necessary for `within` on a
  bytes-only target, never sufficient: a fault that needs attacker bytes AND a
  specific application call order is `outside` on a `bytes` target, because the
  attacker cannot make the application issue that order.
  What is NOT a component: the application's fixed, contract-obeying setup —
  creating or reusing a context, selecting a supported mode or option, and
  calling the documented entry point that consumes the input. Those the
  application always performs, whatever the bytes say. What IS a component: a
  post-parse call order, a specific lifetime or teardown order the application
  chooses, environment, filesystem state, or timing that the fault needs and no
  listed control covers. Answer **unclear** only when source did not settle it
  in budget. Decide this from the code you read, never from the report's
  self-declared `Trigger source`: that field is written by whoever found the bug
  and is unreliable in both directions — a driver exercising documented entry
  points reads as caller-driven even when attacker bytes alone decide the fault,
  and an unreproduced claim reads as byte-driven even when only a caller can
  reach it.
- For Reject, `rejection_kind` distinguishes a false security claim from a
  real target defect that adds no capability at a code-execution or trusted
  operator surface. Use `no-added-boundary` only for the latter; it remains
  visible as a retained engineering defect instead of a security bug.
  Use `consequence-disproved` only when source contradicts the report's exact
  security consequence, not when evidence is missing or a different scenario
  would be required.

Budget: {{ timeout_secs }}s wall time, ~60 tool calls. Be calibrated.
